:: Carter Pool Swimming Marco Polo [nobr]

/* Intro */
<<set $Minutes += 20>>
It is only a matter of time with you both swimming around that one of you would suggest the most common and widely played pool game amongst people of all ages. A game that has gone down in history for being played in every pool, despite or perhaps because of how simple it is. A game no one truly knows the origin of, yet everyone knows the rules. Truly a timeless piece of human history summed up in two words. <br>

    <<set _ad to [
        {
            Name: "Player",
            State: {
                Mouth: "Happy",
            },
        },
        {
            Name: "Carter",
            State: {
                Outfit: "Swim",
                Mouth: "Happy",
            },
        },
    ]>>
    <<ad _ad>>

<span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Hey! Wanna play Marco-Polo?”</span> <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> suggests happily, assuming you know the rules as everyone does, as it is truly a timeless classic. You nod, asking if <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> wants to be Marco or Polo first, though he is a little confused. <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Is Marco the seeker? I want to be the seeker first, so whichever one is that!”</span> You laugh, actually not knowing yourself who Marco or Polo is. <span style="color:Yellow">This game is truly a mystery for the ages.</span> <br>
<br>
Regardless of unanswerable questions, <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> is chosen to be the seeker first and moves to the center of the pool, closing his eyes as you move to one area of the pool, shouting for him to not cheat by peeking. <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“I would never!”</span> He responds, his honor besmirched by your insinuation that he would cheat. Once he is in place, the game of trust and timelessness begins. <br>
<br>
<span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Marco!”</span> <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> calls out happily as you respond in turn. His head snaps towards you, swimming slowly over as he accidentally swallows some pool water when he calls out again, <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Marco!”</span> You move carefully away after each response, careful not to disturb the water too much as he moves closer and quicker yet. <br>
<br>
Though there is one problem: <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> <span style="color:Yellow">is surprisingly bad at this game.</span> It is kind of amusing how, with every Polo response, his echolocation is slightly off. He also tries to move towards you pretty fast, making it easy for you to mask your escape. It is kind of cute to watch him wander around, hitting the edge of the pool over and over as he just misses you. Though you maybe start to feel a little bad that he is having so much trouble. <br>
<br>

<div id="choice">
	<<link "Help Carter<br>">>
    <<replace "#choice">>
        In order to make the game both fairer and more fun, you decide to help <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> by slowing down and allowing yourself to just barely get tagged. This causes the boy to happily jump up <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Finally! I got you! I knew you couldn’t escape me forever!”</span> <span style="color:LightGreen">The boy’s smile and joy at winning is precious and you happily allow him to revile in his victory.</span> <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Alright! You’re turn! You’ll never catch me!”</span> <br>

            <<set _ad to [
                {
                    Name: "Player",
                    State: {
                        Mouth: "Happy",
                    },
                },
                {
                    Name: "Carter",
                    State: {
                        Outfit: "Swim",
                        Mouth: "Happy",
                    },
                },
            ]>>
            <<ad _ad>>
        
        Though it turns out <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> is just as bad at hiding as he is seeking. His movements are slow yet chaotic, making it very easy to hear him when he moves. He also waits to move until you get very close, seemingly thinking that is the best strategy. It leads to you very easily nearly catching him a few times. You even slow down a few times, just to keep the game interesting. The numerous close calls also makes the game more thrilling, as <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> dodges and dives as you move a few inches away from tagging him. <br>
        <br>
        <span style="color:LightGreen">So, despite having to give <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> some help now and again, the overall game is pretty fun and you are both laughing and tired near the end of it.</span> <br>
        <span style="color:green">+Carter Comfort</span><br>
        <<set $HangOutNeed.Comfort += 5>>
        <span style="color:Red">-Carter Energy</span><br>
        <<set $HangOutNeed.Energy -= 10>>
        <span style="color:Green">+Carter Fun</span><br>
        <<set $HangOutNeed.Fun += 10>>
        <<needChange "Fun" 10>>
        <<socialChange "Carter" 1>>
        <br>
        [[Continue|Carter House Backyard Swim]]
    <</replace>>
    <</link>>
	<<link "Watch him<br>">>
    <<replace "#choice">>
        You decide to just watch <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> struggle from the side. Maybe you think this will be the best way for him to learn how to play properly, maybe you respect him too much to give him help out of pity, or maybe you just like watching him suffer. Your reasons are your own as you keep moving slowly and only making noise when he calls out. <br>
        <br>
        <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Marco!”</span> <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> starts to sound very frustrated, not able to get close to you with his clumsy and loud techniques. <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Are you even in the pool still!?”</span> You respond in the affirmative, splashing the water with your hands to ensure him. Still, this does little to ease his frustration as he eventually comes to the sound of your voice so fast that he collides with the pool wall and bangs his elbow. <br>

            <<set _ad to [
                {
                    Name: "Player",
                    State: {
                        Mouth: "Happy",
                    },
                },
                {
                    Name: "Carter",
                    State: {
                        Outfit: "Swim",
                        Mouth: "Frown",
                        Eyes: "Tired",
                        Blush: 1,
                    },
                },
            ]>>
            <<ad _ad>>

        <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Ow!”</span> He cries, opening his eyes to check on his bruised limb before sighing. <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Alright, you win, let’s just play something else.”</span> <span style="color:Yellow">It seems the constant losing has caused <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Carter</span> to want to abandon the game. You feel a little bad, but not enough to actually let him win. Maybe next time.</span> <br>
        <span style="color:Red">-Carter Comfort</span><br>
        <<set $HangOutNeed.Comfort -= 10>>
        <span style="color:Red">-Carter Energy</span><br>
        <<set $HangOutNeed.Energy -= 10>>
        <span style="color:Red">-Carter Fun</span><br>
        <<set $HangOutNeed.Fun -= 20>>
        <<needChange "Fun" 5>>
        <<socialChange "Carter" 1>>
        <br>
        [[Continue|Carter House Backyard Swim]]
    <</replace>>
    <</link>>
</div>